@*
    A button that is UNAVAILABLE rather than ABSENT.

    Native `disabled` removes a control from the tab order and from the screen
    reader's button list. For a sighted user that is fine — the greyed-out button is
    still on screen, still says what it does, and its position tells them which field
    to go back and fix. For the user this product exists for, `disabled` is deletion:
    type 0 into the order ticket's Quantity and "Submit Buy Order" simply stops
    existing, with nothing said, and there is no way to find out why. That was
    demonstrated on 2026-09-02 — one bUnit test, quantity 0, the submit button
    `disabled=True` and the quantity field carrying no invalid state at all.

    So this renders a button that is never natively disabled:

      * `aria-disabled="true"` while blocked — every screen reader announces
        "unavailable" / "dimmed", so the control is still THERE and still says its
        name, which is what tells the user a path exists at all;
      * the reason is a real sentence in a visually-hidden span wired to
        `aria-describedby`, so tabbing to the button announces name, state and cause
        together — the one channel that reaches the user at every verbosity level and
        in braille, without depending on a live region that may have been interrupted;
      * activating it while blocked SPEAKS the reason and does not invoke OnClick.

    THE GATE IS A FUNCTION, NOT A BOOL, and that is the whole safety argument.
    `aria-disabled` stops nothing in the DOM: unlike `disabled`, the browser will
    happily deliver a second click. A cached `Blocked` parameter is only as fresh as
    the last render, so a double-Enter — which the order ticket's in-flight latch
    exists precisely because it once placed two live orders — could reach the handler
    on stale state. Gate() is therefore evaluated AGAIN, synchronously, at click time,
    against the same expression that drew the button. Returning null means "go ahead";
    returning a sentence means "refuse, and here is why", so a gate with several causes
    cannot report the wrong one.

    Callers keep their own guards. `SubmitOrder`'s `if (!CanSubmit) return;` still
    stands behind this; defence in depth, not a replacement.

    Usage:
        <GatedButton class="submit-btn" Gate="SubmitGate" OnClick="SubmitOrder">
            Submit @_orderSide Order
        </GatedButton>

        private string? SubmitGate() =>
            _orderInFlight ? "An order is already being sent."
            : _orderQty <= 0 ? "Enter a quantity above zero."
            : null;
*@
@inject IEventBus EventBus

<button @attributes="Extra"
        type="@Type"
        id="@ElementId"
        aria-disabled="@BlockedAttr"
        aria-describedby="@DescribedByAttr"
        @onclick="ActivateAsync">@ChildContent</button>@*
    Always rendered, and blank when the action is available. A conditionally rendered
    span would leave aria-describedby pointing at nothing — a dangling IDREF, handled
    differently by every screen reader — while a span whose TEXT is conditional reads
    as the empty string, which is announced as nothing at all. And the text must go
    when the gate opens: an enabled Confirm that still said "Enter a quantity above
    zero." after the field was fixed would be worse than saying nothing.

    role="none" because this span is a SIBLING of the button, and some of the buttons
    it follows sit inside a role="menu" (ChartContextMenu) or a role="tablist", whose
    owned children are required to be menuitems / tabs. A presentational element is
    not an owned child, so the container's structure stays conformant — and the text
    is still reachable, because aria-describedby resolves through the text alternative
    computation rather than through the accessibility tree.
*@<span id="@ReasonId" role="none" class="gated-reason">@Reason</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The gate. Returns null when the action can be taken; otherwise the reason it
    /// cannot, as a sentence the user can act on — "Enter a quantity above zero.",
    /// not "Invalid input". Called on every render AND again at click time, so it must
    /// be cheap and side-effect free.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Required at every call site — including sites that cannot be blocked today,
    /// because the gate expression is the thing that changes. A gate that never blocks
    /// is written <c>() =&gt; null</c> and costs nothing.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<string?> Gate { get; set; } = () => null;

    /// <summary>Invoked only when <see cref="Gate"/> returns null at click time.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClick { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which speech channel carries the refusal. Left at Manual — the channel every
    /// other "why did that key do nothing" message uses — for ordinary controls.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Pass <c>SpeechChannel.Critical</c> for anything that spends money.
    /// <c>FeedbackType.Boundary</c> speaks on Manual, which F2 silences, so a muted
    /// terminal would answer a refused order button with an earcon and no account of
    /// why — the same shape as the live-order readback that F2 silenced until
    /// 2026-09-01, on a screen with no visual channel to fall back to.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public SpeechChannel? Channel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Optional DOM id. Set it when something needs to move focus here;
    /// <c>accessibleTrader.focusElement</c> addresses elements by id.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? ElementId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Defaults to "button". Overridable because a button inside a &lt;form&gt;
    /// defaults to submit in HTML and this component must not change that silently.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Type { get; set; } = "button";

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything else the call site wrote — class, aria-label, title, style. Splatted
    /// FIRST so the attributes this component owns (aria-disabled, aria-describedby,
    /// type, id) win over a stray duplicate rather than being silently replaced by one.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? Extra { get; set; }

    private readonly string _reasonId = "gated-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N")[..8];
    private string ReasonId => _reasonId;

    private string? Reason => Gate?.Invoke();

    private string? BlockedAttr => Reason is null ? null : "true";

    /// <summary>
    /// The call site's own aria-describedby is kept and the reason id APPENDED — before
    /// it, because a description is read in listed order and the reason a control is
    /// refused should not arrive behind a paragraph of standing hint text.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// The reason id is listed ONLY while there IS a reason. An aria-describedby that
    /// resolves to the empty string is not "no description" — it is a description of "",
    /// and it suppresses <c>title</c>, which is where a tooltip lives. That was measured
    /// in Chromium on 2026-09-03 and it had silently deleted the spoken tooltip from every
    /// button in <c>ToolbarIconButton</c>, which shares this contract. Since the settings
    /// restructure later that day one call site DOES pass a <c>title</c> — Settings' "Edit
    /// theme" (<c>s-theme-edit</c>, "Change {theme} in place") — so this is no longer only
    /// robustness: while the gate is open the attribute is omitted and the title is the
    /// description; while blocked the reason replaces it.
    /// <c>SettingsModal_NewCloneEdit_OpenTheEditorInTheirOwnModes</c> pins the open half.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Returning null omits the attribute entirely. Nothing dangles either way: the reason
    /// span is rendered whether or not anything points at it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string? DescribedByAttr
    {
        get
        {
            string? own = null;
            if (Extra != null && Extra.TryGetValue("aria-describedby", out var v))
                own = v?.ToString();
            bool blocked = Reason is not null;
            if (!blocked) return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(own) ? null : own;
            return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(own) ? ReasonId : ReasonId + " " + own;
        }
    }

    private async Task ActivateAsync()
    {
        // Re-evaluated here, not read from the last render. See the header.
        var reason = Gate?.Invoke();
        if (reason is not null)
        {
            // Boundary, not Error: nothing has gone wrong, the user has arrived at the
            // edge of what this control will do. Same channel and same earcon as every
            // other refusal in the app, unless the call site raised it.
            EventBus.Publish(new FeedbackRequestEvent(
                FeedbackType.Boundary, reason, Channel: Channel));
            return;
        }
        await OnClick.InvokeAsync();
    }
}
